/// \defgroup grp_w3_random_values Random value streams
/// \ingroup grp_common_services
/// \brief The engine's three random-number streams (logic, client and
///        audio), the one generator they share, and the two functions that
///        expose the logic stream's seed and a checksum of its state.
///
/// The engine's own random streams are reached through six functions: an
/// integer form and a Real form for each stream. All six advance a six-word
/// state with randomValue and map the 32-bit result onto the range the
/// caller asked for. Each stream has its own state, so a draw from one never
/// moves another. The WWLib generator classes
/// (\ref grp_w1_wwlib_random_strutil) are a separate family that W3D
/// presentation code uses. Some presentation and network code calls the C
/// runtime rand() directly, outside these streams;
/// SnowManager::updateIniSettings and GameInfo::reset are examples.
///
/// The split exists for determinism. Zero Hour's source gives the reason:
/// client and logic draws are kept apart so that the game logic stays
/// deterministic whatever effects the client displays. BFME 2 keeps the
/// same arrangement (\ref page_core_frameworks_random):
/// - The logic stream drives the simulation. Every peer in a lockstep game,
///   and every replay, seeds it with the same value and must then draw from
///   it in the same order and the same number of times. GameLogic::getCRC
///   writes GetGameLogicRandomSeedCRC into the game-state CRC, so a machine
///   whose logic stream has drifted fails the next CRC comparison
///   (\ref sub_save_load_crc_crc).
/// - The client stream serves presentation and the audio stream serves
///   sound. Neither stream's state is part of the game-state CRC.
///
/// \par Who draws from which stream
/// These are examples from matched BFME 2 callers, not complete lists:
/// - Logic, integer: ScriptEngine::setTimer, ThingFactory::newObject,
///   WeaponTemplate::getClipReloadTime, LifetimeUpdate::calcSleepDelay,
///   SlowDeathBehavior::onDie, AI states such as AIWanderState,
///   AIPanicState and AIFearState, and game setup
///   (populateRandomSideAndColor, populateRandomStartPosition).
/// - Logic, Real: PartitionManager::findPositionAround,
///   CreateCrateDie::testCreationChance, CreateCrateDie::createCrate,
///   SlavedUpdate::doRepairLogic, StructureCollapseUpdate::update,
///   TunnelContain::scatterToNearbyPosition,
///   GameLogicRandomVariable::getValue and the Lua callback GetRandomNumber.
/// - Client: Anim2D::randomizeCurrentFrame, Drawable::calcPhysicsXformWheels,
///   ActiveBody::createParticleSystems, TerrainScorchFXNugget::doFXPos,
///   W3DView::shake, W3DRopeDraw::buildSegments, SwayClientUpdate::updateSway,
///   GameClientRandomVariable::getValue (which particle emission code
///   samples) and the Lua callback GetClientRandomNumberReal.
/// - Audio: AudioEventRTS::generatePlayInfo,
///   MilesAudioManager::addResumeOrPushMultisound and
///   MilesAudioManager::playAndStoreStream.
///
/// \par Seeding
/// InitRandom seeds all three streams, and InitGameLogicRandom reseeds only
/// the logic stream. Before seeding, both read a seed field in the global
/// data. When the global data exists and that field is not -1, its value
/// replaces the requested seed. The -randomSeed command-line option writes
/// that field. InitRandom without an argument takes its seed from the C
/// runtime time(0) and passes it to the seeded InitRandom, so the same
/// override applies there. Seeding sets each of a stream's six words to the
/// seed plus a fixed constant, the same six constants for every stream.
/// Both functions record the seed they used, after any override, and
/// GetGameLogicRandomSeed returns it.
/// \ref page_core_frameworks_random describes when they run.
///
/// Zero Hour stores a similar fixed seed, set by its -seed option or the
/// FixedSeed key of GameData.ini, but its InitRandom and InitGameLogicRandom
/// do not read it. BFME 2's GameData INI block still accepts a FixedSeed
/// field, parsed as an integer, but it fills a different global-data field
/// from the one these functions read. What reads that field is not
/// established.
///
/// \par Where the code lives
/// In BFME 2's WorldBuilder debug build, the assert paths of the two logic
/// forms and of the client and audio Real forms name Common/RandomValue.cpp.
/// The other functions have no asserts in that build, but their WorldBuilder
/// copies sit in the same stretch of code: randomValue and the client and
/// audio integer forms lie between asserted functions, and
/// GetGameLogicRandomSeed and GetGameLogicRandomSeedCRC lie shortly before
/// the first asserted one, with the InitRandom and InitGameLogicRandom
/// copies in between. Zero Hour's RandomValue.cpp defines all of them, so
/// they very likely belong to the same unit. The reconstruction splits the
/// unit into several small files. Where one of them declares its own static
/// copy of a seed array, read it as that stream's single retail array: each
/// split file's copy stands for the same retail global. This is the data
/// counterpart of a private class view (\ref page_reading_code_views).
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Nothing in these functions is data-driven. The generator, the starting
///   constants and the range arithmetic are fixed. Data reaches them only
///   through their callers. Random-variable fields, such as those that
///   INI::parseGameClientRandomVariable reads, are sampled by
///   GameClientRandomVariable::getValue or GameLogicRandomVariable::getValue,
///   and Lua scripts call GetRandomNumber (logic stream) or
///   GetClientRandomNumberReal (client stream).
/// - Any change to how many logic draws happen shifts every later logic
///   value, and a change in their order hands the values to different
///   callers. That covers code changes and data changes that send logic
///   code down a different path. Peers running the unchanged game then
///   desync, which the CRC check reports, and replays recorded before the
///   change no longer play back the same way.
/// - Presentation-only code must use the client or audio stream. Client code
///   does not run in lockstep, so a logic draw made there can happen a
///   different number of times on each machine.
/// - The -randomSeed command-line option forces the seed: InitRandom and
///   InitGameLogicRandom then use its value instead of the one they were
///   given. With a forced seed, logic random values repeat from run to run
///   as long as the same draws happen in the same order, which helps when
///   reproducing a problem. The override also replaces the seed a network
///   game start passes in, so a peer started with it while the others are
///   not will normally desync. The FixedSeed field of the GameData INI
///   block is not the same setting in BFME 2: it fills a field these
///   functions do not read (see Seeding above).
/// - In BFME 2's WorldBuilder debug build, both logic forms assert
///   "Invalid call during non-logic phase" when a GameLogic exists and is
///   not inside its logic update. Retail has no such check, so a misplaced
///   logic draw shows up only as a desync.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - A port that must stay compatible with retail peers or replays has to
///   reproduce randomValue and the range mapping bit for bit: unsigned
///   32-bit wraparound, the modulo mapping of the integer forms and the
///   single-precision arithmetic of the Real forms
///   (\ref page_core_frameworks_remastering covers floating point).
/// - Apart from the C runtime strrchr and the game's own log-append helper,
///   both used only in the logic forms' log path, the draw functions call
///   nothing outside this family. InitRandom without an argument seeds from
///   the C runtime time(0).
/// - randomValue relies on UnsignedInt being exactly 32 bits with
///   wraparound. That holds on mainstream 32- and 64-bit compilers, but a
///   fixed-width type such as uint32_t makes the assumption explicit.
/// - Only the logic stream's state enters the game-state CRC.

/// \file Common/RandomValue_randomValue.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w3_random_values
/// \brief randomValue, the generator shared by all three streams.
///
/// One of the small files the reconstruction splits RandomValue.cpp into;
/// the original unit is inferred from its neighbours and Zero Hour (see
/// \ref grp_w3_random_values).

/// \file Common/GameLogicRandomValue_getValueInt.cpp
