/// \file Common/GameLogicRandomSeed_get.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w3_random_values
/// \brief GetGameLogicRandomSeed, which returns the logic stream's base seed.
///
/// One of the small files the reconstruction splits RandomValue.cpp into;
/// the original unit is inferred from its neighbours and Zero Hour (see
/// \ref grp_w3_random_values).

/// \file Common/GameLogicRandomSeedCRC_get.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w3_random_values
/// \brief GetGameLogicRandomSeedCRC, the checksum of the logic stream's
///        state.
///
/// One of the small files the reconstruction splits RandomValue.cpp into;
/// the original unit is inferred from its neighbours and Zero Hour (see
/// \ref grp_w3_random_values).

/// \fn UnsignedInt randomValue(UnsignedInt *seed)
/// \brief Advances one stream's six-word state and returns the next 32-bit
///        random value.
///
/// The single generator behind all three streams. Each of the six draw
/// functions passes its own stream's state, and they are its only direct
/// callers in retail.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The state is six 32-bit words. The function first runs an add-with-carry
/// chain from the last word to the first: the last two words are added and
/// the sum replaces the fifth word, then the running sum plus the carry is
/// added to each earlier word in turn, and each new sum replaces the word it
/// was added to. It then increments the whole array as one 192-bit counter
/// whose least significant word is the last, carrying upward. The return
/// value is the new first word.
///
/// \param seed A stream's six-word state, updated in place. Must not be
///             null.
/// \return The next value, any unsigned 32-bit number.
///
/// \note Zero Hour declares this function static in RandomValue.cpp. In
///       BFME 2 retail it takes the state pointer in a register, so the
///       reconstruction declares it __fastcall to match.
/// \see GetGameLogicRandomValue, GetGameLogicRandomValueReal,
///      GetGameClientRandomValue, GetGameAudioRandomValue

/// \fn Int GetGameLogicRandomValue(Int lo, Int hi, char *file, int line)
/// \brief Draws an integer from lo to hi inclusive from the logic stream,
///        which every lockstep peer must draw identically.
///
/// The integer draw for simulation code: AI states, script timers, weapon
/// reload times, object creation and game setup all use it (see the group
/// description). Zero Hour code calls it through the GameLogicRandomValue
/// macro, which supplies the caller's file name and line. Each BFME 2
/// retail call site pushes its caller's source path, as it was on the
/// original build machine, and line number as constants, which is what such
/// a macro produces. A matched caller in the reconstruction must push the
/// same values, whether it passes them explicitly or through a macro.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - The range size hi - lo + 1 is computed as an unsigned 32-bit value.
///   When it wraps to zero (hi is one below lo, or lo and hi are the most
///   negative and most positive Int values), the function returns hi
///   without drawing.
/// - Otherwise it takes one value from randomValue, reduces it modulo the
///   range size and adds lo. When the range size is not a power of two the
///   modulo favours smaller results very slightly; the bias is negligible
///   for small ranges.
/// - When the logic-random log object is set, the function also appends a
///   line "logicrandom = <value> (<file>, <line>)" to it, giving only the
///   part of file after its last backslash.
///
/// The log object holds a list of text lines. The line is formatted and
/// appended through a game-side format-and-append helper, not the C runtime
/// fprintf, although the reconstruction spells the call that way.
/// WorldBuilder's debug build names that helper Append, a method of a class
/// it calls CRCParameterCheck. While the object is set, GameLogic::getCRC
/// also feeds its lines into the game-state CRC, in a block that retail
/// labels with the same class name, and then clears them. What creates or
/// enables the object in BFME 2 is not established.
///
/// \param lo   Lowest value to return.
/// \param hi   Highest value to return. Must not be below lo: a reversed
///             range is not swapped, and the result then falls outside it.
/// \param file Caller's source path. Only the log reads it. The log takes
///             the text after the last backslash and does not handle a path
///             without one.
/// \param line Caller's source line, for the log.
/// \return A value from lo to hi inclusive, or hi when the range size wraps
///         to zero.
///
/// \note Zero Hour logs logic draws only in builds that define its
///       debug-random switch, through its debug log. BFME 2 retail keeps the
///       log append in release code, as BFME 1 does.
/// \see GetGameLogicRandomValueReal, GetGameClientRandomValue, randomValue,
///      GetGameLogicRandomSeedCRC

/// \fn Real GetGameLogicRandomValueReal(Real lo, Real hi, char *file, int line)
/// \brief Draws a Real from lo to hi from the logic stream.
///
/// The floating-point twin of GetGameLogicRandomValue, for positions,
/// offsets, chances and delays in the simulation.
/// GameLogicRandomVariable::getValue calls it for its uniform distribution,
/// and the Lua callback GetRandomNumber exposes it to scripts. Every draw
/// counts toward the lockstep contract in the same way as the integer form.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - If hi - lo is zero or negative, the function returns hi without
///   drawing.
/// - Otherwise one value from randomValue is converted to Real and
///   multiplied by a constant of about 2^-32, written as 1 / (2^32 - 1), to
///   give a fraction from 0 to 1. The fraction is scaled by hi - lo and lo
///   is added. Nothing excludes hi: the WorldBuilder debug build asserts
///   that the result lies from lo to hi inclusive.
/// - When the logic-random log object is set, the function appends
///   "logicrandom = <value> (<file>, <line>)" to it, exactly as
///   GetGameLogicRandomValue does.
///
/// \param lo   Lower end of the range.
/// \param hi   Upper end of the range. When it is not above lo, hi is
///             returned and no value is drawn.
/// \param file Caller's source path, for the log only. The log takes the
///             text after the last backslash and does not handle a path
///             without one.
/// \param line Caller's source line, for the log.
/// \return A value from lo to hi, or hi when the range is empty or
///         reversed.
///
/// \note The result depends on single-precision rounding, so a port must
///       reproduce the floating-point behaviour to stay in step with retail
///       (\ref page_core_frameworks_remastering).
/// \see GetGameLogicRandomValue, GetGameClientRandomValueReal, randomValue

/// \fn Int GetGameClientRandomValue(Int lo, Int hi, char *file, int line)
/// \brief Draws an integer from lo to hi inclusive from the client stream,
///        for presentation that may differ between machines.
///
/// Used by drawing and effects code: animation frames, wheel motion,
/// particle-system creation, terrain scorch marks and similar choices (see
/// the group description). Drawing here never disturbs the logic stream.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The same arithmetic as GetGameLogicRandomValue on the client state: an
/// unsigned range size hi - lo + 1, hi returned without a draw when that
/// size wraps to zero, otherwise one randomValue result modulo the size plus
/// lo. There is no log; file and line are unused in retail.
///
/// \param lo   Lowest value to return.
/// \param hi   Highest value to return. Must not be below lo.
/// \param file Caller's source path. Unused.
/// \param line Caller's source line. Unused.
/// \return A value from lo to hi inclusive, or hi when the range size wraps
///         to zero.
///
/// \note Zero Hour reads file and line only in builds that define its
///       client debug-random switch.
/// \see GetGameClientRandomValueReal, GetGameLogicRandomValue, randomValue

/// \fn Real GetGameClientRandomValueReal(Real lo, Real hi, char *file, int line)
/// \brief Draws a Real from lo to hi from the client stream, for
///        presentation that may differ between machines.
///
/// Callers include camera shake (W3DView::shake), rope and sway animation,
/// particle emission through GameClientRandomVariable::getValue, and the Lua
/// callback GetClientRandomNumberReal. Values drawn here may differ from
/// machine to machine, so they must feed only presentation.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The same arithmetic as GetGameLogicRandomValueReal on the client state:
/// hi is returned without a draw when hi - lo is zero or negative,
/// otherwise a randomValue result scaled to a fraction from 0 to 1, times
/// hi - lo, plus lo. There is no log.
///
/// \param lo   Lower end of the range.
/// \param hi   Upper end of the range. When it is not above lo, hi is
///             returned and no value is drawn.
/// \param file Caller's source path. Unused.
/// \param line Caller's source line. Unused.
/// \return A value from lo to hi, or hi when the range is empty or
///         reversed.
/// \see GetGameClientRandomValue, GetGameLogicRandomValueReal, randomValue

/// \fn Int GetGameAudioRandomValue(Int lo, Int hi, char *file, int line)
/// \brief Draws an integer from lo to hi inclusive from the audio stream.
///
/// Sound code uses it to choose among an event's sounds: callers include
/// MilesAudioManager::addResumeOrPushMultisound and a weighted sound picker
/// that AudioEventRTS::generatePlayInfo uses. Draws from this stream
/// neither move the logic stream nor depend on how many client draws were
/// made.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The same arithmetic as GetGameLogicRandomValue on the audio state, with
/// no log; file and line are unused in retail.
///
/// \param lo   Lowest value to return.
/// \param hi   Highest value to return. Must not be below lo.
/// \param file Caller's source path. Unused.
/// \param line Caller's source line. Unused.
/// \return A value from lo to hi inclusive, or hi when the range size wraps
///         to zero.
///
/// \note Zero Hour: AudioEventRTS::generateFilename uses this form to pick
///       one of an event's sounds, and generatePlayInfo uses it to pick the
///       attack and decay sounds, but only for events not flagged as logical
///       audio. For flagged events Zero Hour draws those choices from the
///       logic stream instead. Zero Hour's header says the flag "should
///       probably only be true for scripted sounds", and a comment beside
///       the attack and decay picks says they need to be logic "because it
///       needs to be the same on all systems". BFME 2's generatePlayInfo
///       picks the attack and decay sounds through the weighted picker on
///       this stream, and its body has no logical-audio branch.
/// \see GetGameAudioRandomValueReal, GetGameClientRandomValue, randomValue

/// \fn Real GetGameAudioRandomValueReal(Real lo, Real hi, char *file, int line)
/// \brief Draws a Real from lo to hi from the audio stream.
///
/// Callers include AudioEventRTS::generatePlayInfo and
/// MilesAudioManager::playAndStoreStream.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The same arithmetic as GetGameLogicRandomValueReal on the audio state,
/// with no log.
///
/// \param lo   Lower end of the range.
/// \param hi   Upper end of the range. When it is not above lo, hi is
///             returned and no value is drawn.
/// \param file Caller's source path. Unused.
/// \param line Caller's source line. Unused.
/// \return A value from lo to hi, or hi when the range is empty or
///         reversed.
///
/// \note Zero Hour: AudioEventRTS::generatePlayInfo draws the pitch shift
///       from the event's pitch-shift minimum and maximum, and the volume
///       shift from 1 plus the event's volume-shift value up to 1.
///       AudioEventRTS::generateFilename draws the delay from the event's
///       delay minimum and maximum. BFME 2's generatePlayInfo also draws the
///       pitch and volume shifts from this stream.
/// \see GetGameAudioRandomValue, GetGameClientRandomValueReal, randomValue

/// \fn UnsignedInt GetGameLogicRandomSeed(void)
/// \brief Returns the seed the logic stream was last seeded with.
///
/// This is the base seed that InitRandom or InitGameLogicRandom stored,
/// after any override those functions apply, not the stream's current
/// state. It therefore stays the same however many logic draws have been
/// made since.
///
/// In BFME 2, populateRandomSideAndColor uses it during game setup: it
/// makes a number of throw-away logic draws equal to the seed modulo 7
/// before choosing each random faction, as Zero Hour's GameLogic.cpp does.
/// Zero Hour's Recorder also stores this value in the game information it
/// records for a replay; that use is not confirmed for BFME 2.
///
/// \return The stored base seed.
/// \see GetGameLogicRandomSeedCRC, GetGameLogicRandomValue

/// \fn UnsignedInt GetGameLogicRandomSeedCRC(void)
/// \brief Returns a checksum of the logic stream's current state, for the
///        game-state CRC.
///
/// Unlike GetGameLogicRandomSeed this hashes the live six-word state, which
/// changes with every logic draw. Machines that seeded alike and made the
/// same number of logic draws get the same value; a machine that drew a
/// different number of times will normally get a different one.
/// GameLogic::getCRC writes the value into the game-state CRC
/// (\ref sub_save_load_crc_crc), which is how a divergence in logic draws
/// is caught.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The state's bytes are passed to ComputeCRC with a starting value of 0.
/// For each byte the running value is shifted left by one bit, the byte is
/// added, and the bit shifted out is added back in.
///
/// \return The checksum of the current logic state.
///
/// \note Zero Hour computes the same per-byte sum with its CRC class. BFME 2
///       calls the free function ComputeCRC, which retail's network code
///       (for example Transport::queueSend) also calls.
/// \see GetGameLogicRandomSeed, randomValue
